<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yasodharman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 530</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Yashodharman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yasodharman was a ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the early 6th century, generally assigned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulikara-dynasty">Aulikara dynasty</a>. His inscriptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandsaur">Mandsaur</a> claim that he defeated the Alchon Hun king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a>, around 528, and ruled lands from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> to the sea. His power appears to have been short-lived, and nothing is known of his successors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yasodharman.jpg" alt="Yasodharman" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ambrose Dudley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_defeat_of_the_Ephalites,_or_White_Huns_A.D._528.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Yashodharman belonged to the Second Aulikara dynasty and ruled the Malava kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-india">North India</a>. Towards the end of the 5th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> had come under attack from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Hunas</a>. Yashodharman, and possibly also the Gupta emperor Narasimhagupta, defeated a Huna army and its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a> and drove them out of India. Following his victories he occupied most of the territories of the Alchon Huns to the north and most of those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> to the east.</p><p>In line 5 of his pillar inscription Yashodharman claims to rule the land from the neighbourhood of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Lauhitya</a>, the Brahmaputra, to the Western Ocean, and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> to Mount Mahendra. He thus took vast territories from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Hunas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a>, but his empire was short-lived and in the end disintegrated. Only one Gupta inscription is known from after his victories, a land grant of the last Gupta emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnugupta">Vishnugupta</a>, in the area of Kotivarsha, at Bangarh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Three inscriptions of Yashodharman have been found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandsaur">Mandsaur</a>, the ancient Dashapura. One, the stone inscription of Yashodharman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a>, is dated to samvat 589, or 532 CE, and records the building of a well by a man named Daksha at Dashapura during his rule. It praises victories over the mighty kings of the east and many kings of the north, won by peaceful overtures and by war, through which the lineage bearing the Aulikara crest was raised ever higher and the ruler bore the title Supreme King of Kings and Supreme Lord.</p><p>Yashodharman also set up twin monolithic pillars at Sondani in Mandsaur District as a record of his victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Hunas</a>. In their inscription he praises himself for that defeat: even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a>, it says, paid respect to his two feet with flowers from the lock of hair on his head, his forehead pained from being bent low by the strength of Yashodharman's arm in obeisance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yashodharman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The peripheral feudatories that the decentralised Gupta system had cultivated now asserted independence and gave no assistance; it was the Aulikara king <b>Yashodharman</b>, not a Gupta, who claimed the decisive victory over Mihirakula around 528.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Mihirakula was defeated around 528 by the Indian king <b>Yasodharman</b> and possibly by the Guptas, and Alchon power retreated to Kashmir and Gandhara.</blockquote><a href="/state/alchon-huns">Alchon Huns</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 528 <b>Yashodharman</b> set up victory pillars at Sondani near Mandsaur, claiming to have defeated the Hun ruler Mihirakula and to rule lands never held by the Guptas.</blockquote><a href="/state/aulikara-dynasty">Aulikara dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
